To find the “standard deviation” (more precisely, the **standard error**) of the sampling distribution of the sample mean under the null hypothesis, you divide the sample’s standard deviation by the square root of the sample size:

\[
SE = \frac{s}{\sqrt{n}}
    = \frac{1.4}{\sqrt{40}}
    \approx \frac{1.4}{6.3249}
    \approx 0.22 .
\]

So the estimated standard error of the mean when testing \(H_0:\mu = 98.6\) is roughly **0.22**.
